---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { wakeRecords, wakeRuns } from '../../lib/wake';

const observationCount = wakeRecords.reduce((sum, record) => sum + record.observations.length, 0);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Wake methodology"
  description="How Rough at Sea selects, follows, corrects, and publishes longitudinal Wake records."
>
  <article class="methodology">
    <header>
      <div class="meta-row">
        <a href="/wake/">&larr; The Wake</a>
        <span>Pilot methodology · Version 1</span>
      </div>
      <p class="eyebrow">How the observatory works</p>
      <h1>Methodology</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The Wake records the distance between first notice and later knowledge. Its purpose is not
        to prove that Rough at Sea was early, nor to declare that lightly covered stories were
        suppressed. It preserves what was known, what remained uncertain, and what happened next.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="principles-grid" aria-label="Core principles">
      <article class="glass-panel">
        <strong>01</strong>
        <h2>Observation before hindsight</h2>
        <p>Point-zero packets are frozen historical observations. Later knowledge is appended rather than used to rewrite the beginning.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="glass-panel">
        <strong>02</strong>
        <h2>Evidence is not attention</h2>
        <p>A claim can be well supported and little noticed, or widely repeated and weakly supported. Those dimensions remain separate.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="glass-panel">
        <strong>03</strong>
        <h2>Repetition is not corroboration</h2>
        <p>Articles derived from the same paper, press release, wire story, interview, or announcement share an evidentiary lineage.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="glass-panel">
        <strong>04</strong>
        <h2>Missing beats fabricated</h2>
        <p>An unknown date, count, or metric remains empty. Precise-looking guesses do more damage than an honest gap.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">The pipeline</p>
      <h2>From Soundings to an active record</h2>
      <ol class="pipeline">
        <li>
          <span>Daily capture</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Morning Soundings chooses what matters today</h3>
            <p>
              Editorial importance comes first. Longitudinal potential is only a tiebreaker between
              otherwise comparable subjects. Every signal and deep sounding receives a structured
              sidecar packet, even when it is unlikely to become an active Wake record.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Point zero</span>
          <div>
            <h3>The initial state is preserved</h3>
            <p>
              The packet records a narrow claim, supported facts, uncertainty, source roles,
              evidentiary lineages, open questions, tracking terms, and concrete future evidence.
              Contemporary packets are created during publication; the earliest six were explicitly
              reconstructed and remain labeled as such.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Weekly admission</span>
          <div>
            <h3>A small collection is chosen deliberately</h3>
            <p>
              The weekly process looks for stable subjects with consequential unresolved questions,
              observable future evidence, and durable sources. It ordinarily admits no more than one
              to three new records and may admit none. Deferred candidates remain visible in run logs.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Longitudinal review</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Records are reviewed by cadence and event</h3>
            <p>
              A record is revisited when its date is due, a named milestone occurs, a new Soundings
              signal continues it, or credible new evidence appears. Not every record is researched
              every week. A performed review with no material change can still be valuable data.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>Append-only change</span>
          <div>
            <h3>New observations do not erase old ones</h3>
            <p>
              Developments, disagreements, corrections, resolutions, and informed no-change reviews
              become dated observations. The current state mirrors the latest observation, while the
              full timeline preserves how the assessment evolved.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">Three independent readings</p>
      <h2>Lifecycle, evidence, and attention</h2>
      <div class="dimension-grid">
        <article>
          <h3>Lifecycle</h3>
          <p>What is happening to the subject or claim.</p>
          <dl>
            <div><dt>Emerging</dt><dd>Meaningful future evidence is expected.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Corroborated</dt><dd>Important parts gained independent support.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Disputed</dt><dd>Material evidence or interpretation remains contested.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Implemented</dt><dd>A promised program, product, policy, or design was deployed.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Stalled</dt><dd>Expected progress stopped without clear resolution.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Contradicted</dt><dd>Later evidence substantially weakened the original claim.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Resolved</dt><dd>The central open question has a defensible answer.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Dormant</dt><dd>No useful update is expected soon.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Retracted</dt><dd>The source or claim was formally withdrawn.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Evidence</h3>
          <p>What kind and breadth of support currently exist.</p>
          <dl>
            <div><dt>Primary supported</dt><dd>Durable primary evidence supports the bounded claim.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Mixed</dt><dd>Several kinds of evidence exist, with meaningful qualifications.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>First-party only</dt><dd>The claim still rests on an interested source.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Preliminary</dt><dd>Early evidence exists but replication, review, or scale is missing.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Disputed</dt><dd>Qualified sources materially disagree.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Synthesis</dt><dd>The record joins several results rather than following one bounded claim.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Attention</h3>
          <p>How much reproducibly measured public coverage the subject receives.</p>
          <dl>
            <div><dt>Unmeasured</dt><dd>No defensible quantitative attention method has been applied.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Low to high</dt><dd>Reserved for a future reproducible corpus and fixed method.</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Surging or declining</dt><dd>Reserved for measured change between comparable snapshots.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="warning">
            Ordinary search-result estimates are not used. They are unstable, personalized,
            incompletely deduplicated, and often count one syndicated lineage as many reports.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lineage-section glass-panel">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Evidentiary ancestry</p>
        <h2>Why source lineage matters</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Ten articles can appear to corroborate a claim while all ten paraphrase one press release.
          The Wake assigns those sources the same lineage. Independent support requires a genuinely
          different route to the fact: another experiment, another dataset, direct observation,
          independent documentation, or reporting based on separate evidence.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lineage does not make a source bad. A primary paper and the university article explaining it
          can both be useful. It prevents publication count from impersonating evidentiary diversity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">Selection limits</p>
      <h2>What The Wake does not claim</h2>
      <div class="limits">
        <p><strong>It is not exhaustive.</strong> The collection reflects what Morning Soundings found and what the weekly process could responsibly follow.</p>
        <p><strong>Low coverage is not proof of suppression.</strong> Sparse attention may have many causes and is not assigned a motive without evidence.</p>
        <p><strong>Admission is not endorsement.</strong> A record may be followed precisely because an important claim is uncertain or disputed.</p>
        <p><strong>Status is not a quality score.</strong> A disputed record can be more informative than a corroborated one.</p>
        <p><strong>A review is not a demand for novelty.</strong> “No material change” is acceptable when a real search and source check occurred.</p>
        <p><strong>Automation does not remove judgment.</strong> The schemas constrain what must be recorded, but evidence and significance still require accountable editorial decisions.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">Corrections</p>
      <h2>The record must be allowed to embarrass us</h2>
      <p class="wide-copy">
        When an earlier observation contains a factual error, it is not silently rewritten. A
        correction points to the affected observation, explains what changed, cites the supporting
        evidence, and appends a new timeline entry. Typographical repairs that do not alter meaning
        may be fixed normally; substantive history remains visible.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="pilot-state glass-panel">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Current pilot</p>
        <h2>{wakeRecords.length} records · {observationCount} observations · {wakeRuns.length} run</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        The first run is a bootstrap admission ledger, not a retrospective claim that all seven
        subjects changed after publication. Attention remains unmeasured. The public interface and
        both downloads are generated from the same records that the weekly task updates.
      </p>
    </section>

    <footer>
      <a href="/wake/">Explore the active records</a>
      <a href="/wake/data.json" download>Download JSON</a>
      <a href="/wake/data.csv" download>Download CSV</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  :global(main.container) { max-width: 1060px; }
  .methodology { padding-bottom: 5rem; }
  header { margin: 0 auto 4rem; max-width: 60rem; }
  .meta-row { color: var(--text-secondary); display: flex; font-size: 0.75rem; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .meta-row a:hover { color: var(--link-color); }
  .eyebrow { color: var(--link-color); font-size: 0.67rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.14em; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; text-transform: uppercase; }
  h1 { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(3.4rem, 10vw, 6.5rem); letter-spacing: -0.05em; line-height: 0.95; }
  h2 { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.2rem); letter-spacing: -0.03em; }
  .lede { color: var(--text-secondary); font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.08rem, 2.4vw, 1.32rem); line-height: 1.75; margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  section { margin-bottom: 5rem; }
  .principles-grid { display: grid; gap: 0.8rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .principles-grid article { border-radius: 14px; padding: 1.25rem; }
  .principles-grid strong { color: var(--link-color); font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 2.4rem; font-weight: 400; }
  .principles-grid h2 { font-size: 1.35rem; margin-top: 0.5rem; }
  .principles-grid p, .pipeline p, .dimension-grid p, .lineage-section p, .wide-copy, .pilot-state p { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .pipeline { list-style: none; margin: 1.5rem 0 0; padding: 0; }
  .pipeline li { border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color); display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: 10rem minmax(0, 1fr); padding: 1.25rem 0; }
  .pipeline li > span { color: var(--link-color); font-size: 0.68rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.09em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .pipeline h3 { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.35rem; }
  .pipeline p { line-height: 1.7; margin: 0; }
  .dimension-grid { display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .dimension-grid > article { border-top: 2px solid var(--link-color); padding-top: 1rem; }
  .dimension-grid h3 { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.75rem; }
  dl { display: grid; gap: 0.75rem; margin-top: 1rem; }
  dt { font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 700; }
  dd { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 0.75rem; margin: 0.1rem 0 0; }
  .warning { border-left: 2px solid var(--border-color); font-size: 0.75rem; margin-top: 1.25rem; padding-left: 0.8rem; }
  .lineage-section, .pilot-state { border-radius: 16px; display: grid; gap: 2rem; grid-template-columns: minmax(14rem, 0.75fr) minmax(0, 1.25fr); padding: clamp(1.3rem, 4vw, 2rem); }
  .lineage-section p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .limits { display: grid; gap: 0.75rem; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .limits p { border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color); color: var(--text-secondary); margin: 0; padding: 1rem 0; }
  .limits strong { color: var(--text-primary); }
  .wide-copy { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.12rem; line-height: 1.8; max-width: 52rem; }
  .pilot-state { align-items: center; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .pilot-state h2 { font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4vw, 2.5rem); }
  footer { border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color); display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.75rem 1.5rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; }
  footer a { color: var(--link-color); font-size: 0.75rem; }
  @media (max-width: 780px) {
    .dimension-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .lineage-section, .pilot-state { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 580px) {
    .principles-grid, .limits { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .pipeline li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .meta-row { align-items: flex-start; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.45rem; }
  }
</style>
